      detailedWalkthrough: ['The key is to follow the state of the algorithm from initialization to the final result. The steps below explain not only what to do, but why each operation is useful.','If `digits` is empty, return an empty list.','This is the decision point: evaluate the condition using the state built so far. Taking the correct branch prevents invalid work and keeps the algorithm aligned with the problem\'s constraints.','Compute `total`, the product of letter counts.','This extracts the information needed to decide what to do next. The check connects the current input to the condition the problem asks us to satisfy.','For each `idx` in `[0, total)`, decode one letter per digit from the end, using `letters.charAt(idx % len)` and then `idx /= len`.','The traversal systematically processes the relevant elements or states. Each iteration advances the search or updates the maintained state, so the same work is not needlessly repeated.'],
      correctnessCheck: 'The solution is correct when the maintained state contains exactly the information required by the problem and every processed element has been handled according to the stated rule. The final step reads or constructs the answer from that state.',
      code: "import java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.List;\n\nclass Solution {\n    private static final String[] MAP = {\"\", \"\", \"abc\", \"def\", \"ghi\", \"jkl\", \"mno\", \"pqrs\", \"tuv\", \"wxyz\"};\n\n    public List<String> letterCombinations(String digits) {\n        List<String> result = new ArrayList<>();\n        int n = digits.length();\n        if (n == 0) return result;\n        int total = 1;\n        for (char d : digits.toCharArray()) total *= MAP[d - '0'].length();\n        for (int idx = 0; idx < total; idx++) {\n            char[] buf = new char[n];\n            int x = idx;\n            for (int pos = n - 1; pos >= 0; pos--) {\n                String letters = MAP[digits.charAt(pos) - '0'];\n                buf[pos] = letters.charAt(x % letters.length());\n                x /= letters.length();\n            }\n            result.add(new String(buf));\n        }\n        return result;\n    }\n}",
